<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stanisław Żółkiewski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1547">1547</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stanisław Żółkiewski was a Polish military commander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>. In 1610 he defeated a larger Russian force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klushino">Battle of Klushino</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> treat this as the military high point of the Commonwealth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stanis-aw-zo-kiewski.jpg" alt="Stanisław Żółkiewski" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanis%C5%82aw_%C5%BB%C3%B3%C5%82kiewski_11.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stanisław Żółkiewski was born in the village of Turynka, now in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, the son of the voivode of Ruthenia, Stanisław Żółkiewski of the Lubicz coat of arms, and Zofia Lipska. He attended a cathedral school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, had no higher education and did not travel abroad, but studied history on his own. In 1566 he joined the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> as an aide to the royal secretary Jan Zamoyski, and in 1573 he took part in the mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to meet the newly elected king Henry III.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Żółkiewski commanded a company of hussars under King Stefan Batory in the Danzig rebellion and fought in his Livonian campaign. On the night of 11 to 12 May 1584 he captured Samuel Zborowski, whose execution caused controversy. At the Battle of Byczyna in 1588 he led the right flank for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> and received a knee wound that lamed him for life, and he was made Field Crown Hetman.</p><p>He later defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, took part in the Moldavian campaign and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> uprising of Severyn Nalivaiko the year after. He had promised the surrendered Cossack leaders fair treatment, but Nalivaiko was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and a mob of Polish soldiers massacred the other prisoners, worsening Polish-Cossack relations. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> he fought in the Polish-Swedish War.</p><p>In the Polish-Russian War he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-klushino">Battle of Klushino</a> in 1610 against combined Russian and Swedish forces, then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and captured the tsar Vasiliy Shuisky and his brothers. He backed the election of Władysław as tsar and a union of the two states, but Sigismund rejected his plan, which would have required Władysław to convert to Orthodoxy.</p><p>In 1618 he received the office of Grand Crown Hetman, and soon after that of Grand Crown Chancellor. He died on 7 October 1620 during the retreat after the Battle of Cecora against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, reportedly refusing to retreat and staying with the rear guard. His head was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as a trophy, and his widow bought back his body.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Żółkiewski wrote memoirs of the Muscovite war, Początek i progres wojny moskiewskiej, published in 1612 and critical of Sigismund's policies. In 1616 he proposed a larger standing army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, but the parliament did not approve it. He was buried in St Lawrence Church in Żółkiew, the town he enlarged. His son Jan raised a monument at the site of his death in 1621, inscribed with a line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>. Poland remembers him as a military hero, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Unity Day marks the surrender of the Polish garrison he had installed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stanisław Żółkiewski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The high point of Commonwealth arms came in 1610, when Hetman <b>Stanisław Żółkiewski</b> defeated a larger Russian and Swedish force at the Battle of Klushino and Polish troops occupied Moscow, where a group of boyars elected Sigismund III’s son Władysław, the future Władysław IV, as Tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Turynka, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Țuțora, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.14,24.06&amp;year=1594" aria-label="Turynka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stanisław Żółkiewski"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4675 5825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4675 5825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.14,24.06&amp;year=1594">What was here in 1594? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
